Based on screenshots, Unturned may look like another Minecraft with more weapons than just bow, sword or axe where you also have to survive in the post-apocalyptic world full of zombies, which basically means much less mobs than in Minecraft. Is it really like Minecraft? Luckily, I have to deny it. The only thing that those two games have similar is the way graphics are presented. Both of these games focus on the models being made out of blocks, but don't let that deceive any of you. Unturned is like DayZ, but with more weapons or items in general, more vehicles, better crafting system, better multiplayer. Apart from all of these, something that REALLY matters are maps that force the player to focus on the specific type of items or vehicles. The map can force you to look for clothes, food, weapon, ammo or specific vehicle that will allow you to move across the map relatively fast. All of that created by ONE person and the optimisation is fantastic! Did I say that it gets updated frequently?
Pros:
-Variety of maps that force you to specific decisions;
-Lots of items that will let you look however you'd like to look;
-Great choice of weaponry that can be modified with tons of mods, allowing you to create a weapon that suits your playstyle;
-LOADS OF VEHICLES!!! Seriously, if you look for the survival game that has a lot of vehicles, Unturned is for you!
-Multiplayer that functions well in case of both co-operation and rivalisation.
-Fantastic optimalisation (bear in mind that I'm talking about one person, not team of people);
-Lots to do, even when playing solo!
Cons:
-Depending on where you are coming from, the community can be so toxic, that you can get cancer;
-Skins and case system, but it's not a big con as it's not as "offensive" as in other games with this system, giving CS:GO as a simple example;
-Some little bugs that can hinder the pleasure coming from playing the game.
Rating: 9.3/10
The game would get 10/10, but there are some little problems that can get pretty annoying after some time.

Been playing for two weeks; game is great but might be discouraging to first-time players.  Before you get used to it, it can be very frustrating.  I still think stress piles up WAY too high at times (though at others I can use the same team over a month and have no problems -- veyr random).  Diseases/quirks are a pain as far as removal.  You just have to get used to having no way back if you make a mistake.
Things that do not make sense:
--no stress reduction for successfully finishing a mission;
--no stress reduction for ALL heroes when one hero kills a mob;
--multi-target skills grant no stress reduction when just one of the targets dies (oops, yes they do!);
--healers hardly deal a finishing blow and hence have the most stress.  This is where stress reduction from first two bullets would help;
--when mobs die from bleed/blight, no stress reduction;
--too much left up to fate -- some party mix ups simply cannot work; so if you are not lucky enough to get decent heroes on your third week, you may be screwed and have to start over;
--also sucks that all heroes have random skills unlocked; sometimes you are lucky and get all the great skills and breeze through the first few weeks.  Other times you delete.  You delete.  You delete;
--perhaps if heroes are too high for a mission, their resolve can be scaled down somehow; meaning, you can still use your higher level heroes to fill out a lower level party, let's say without them unbalancing it.
--why can't you sell trinkets? (Yes, you can!  Accidentally clicked and sold.)
--why can't you get money back or keep the provisions you did not use up during a mission?
Tiny things:
--should be able to use another torch without going into inv.

